as many as 150 people appeared dead in  northern  india after part of a glacier broke off  and crashed into a dam  in uttarakhand state unleashing floods  waters and debris  eyewitnesses say it happened so fast  that people had almost  no time to react a search for survivors  is underway  amateur video shows the small dam being  swept away by the water and debris  released by the avalanche  [Music]  it then cut further through the area  hitting the hydroelectric power plant in  the valley below  it's feared that people working at the  rishiganga power plant were perhaps  swept away  as well as some villagers  the tragedy happened in the tapovan area  of the northern state of uttarakhand  in northern india this region in the  himalayas is prone to flash floods and  landslides  rescue operations were underway as crews  look for survivors  and tried to secure the area  our correspondent namisha jaiswal is in  delhi following the events in the  district of uttar khan for  us namisha it's difficult to even  imagine these pictures that we're seeing  are truly shocking how dramatic is the  situation  there right now  well rebecca from the updates we're  getting the situation definitely is grim  we have heard about 150 labourers who  were working on one of the power plants  that was hit  another power plant further down has  also been damaged there are also  villages and homes  peppering the banks of the river further  down that could also have been hit  thousands of people from the region have  already been evacuated  teams from the army the border police as  well as disaster response forces have  been deployed  the damage is being assessed and while  authorities claim that flood waters  have already been brought under control  to a certain extent  it's only when all this water actually  recedes that they will be able to assess  the true extent of the damage as well as  the loss of life  yeah you mentioned the flooding there i  mean there's terrifying pictures that  we're seeing  has that flooding really been contained  now or authorities expecting things to  get worse  well so far rebecca authorities are  saying that  what they are insisting on is that  people must not be responding to rumors  about  further flooding or any such or any  determined  information about the loss of life  because they want to ensure that panic  isn't caused  however some information has already  been released saying that further down  the water is below the danger mark so  there is hope that further flooding can  be prevented  further down and there's also dams being  used to  either stall the flow of the water or to  cause for water to be evacuated into a  separate area however the parts of  the river where the water has crashed  through the power  the power generators as well as the dams  have already been severely damaged and  that is where the disaster response  forces will be concentrating  now we know from the i win eyewitness  accounts that it happened so fast that  people didn't really have time  to react but were there any warnings  that something like this might happen  rebecca while there may not have been  immediate warnings there have been  long-term warnings being given by local  uh by local  uh environmentalists as well as  journalists who have pointed out  that an emphasis on tourism in the area  which has led to the whitening of roads  as well as an emphasis on hydel power  projects can lead to disasters and must  be actually be discontinued  the current modi government has actually  been criticized for not coming down more  heavily  on these hydra power plants back in 2013  there was a massive flood episode  in the region the himalayas are after  all an area  that is prone to earthquakes and cloud  bursts and the 2013 episode caused the  loss  of over 5 000 lives and damages to 4 500  villages  so this is of course the region that  does need attention and this disaster  will bring focus back on that fact  all right namisha thanks very much for  the update dw correspondent namisha  j swan in delhi
 

